1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,573
This is MATH 239, video 5 a. The topic is the Principle of Inclusion and Exclusion, from Section 1.1.6 of the notes, and its use for derangements, from Section 1.2.5.

2
00:00:16,023 --> 00:00:22,082
After this video, you can count a union of overlapping sets with Theorem 1.15.

3
00:00:22,532 --> 00:00:27,097
You can explain why the formula counts each element exactly once.

4
00:00:27,547 --> 00:00:30,726
You can count the elements that are in none of the sets.

5
00:00:31,176 --> 00:00:37,405
And you can count surjections and derangements, and solve practice problem P1-7.

6
00:00:37,855 --> 00:00:47,669
Section 1.1.6 starts with this problem. Each flower in a bouquet is fresh, fragrant or colourful, or more than one of these.

7
00:00:48,119 --> 00:00:56,204
We know seven counts, a to g. For example, 11 flowers are fresh, and 2 flowers have all three properties.

8
00:00:56,654 --> 00:00:59,854
The question is: how many flowers are in the bouquet?

9
00:01:00,304 --> 00:01:09,328
Draw one circle for each property: fresh, fragrant and colourful. A flower is inside the circle of each property that it has.

10
00:01:09,778 --> 00:01:14,343
The circles overlap, because one flower can have two or three properties.

11
00:01:14,793 --> 00:01:18,271
If we add 11, 7 and 8, we get 26.

12
00:01:18,721 --> 00:01:24,331
But a flower in two circles is in two of these counts. So the sum counts it 2 times.

13
00:01:24,781 --> 00:01:30,712
A flower in all three circles is counted 3 times. So 26 is too large.

14
00:01:31,162 --> 00:01:46,181
Example 1.14 counts each region of the diagram. The gray letters g to m name the regions. Start at the centre, region g. Item g says that 2 flowers have all three properties.

15
00:01:46,631 --> 00:01:57,361
Next, the regions with exactly two properties. Item f says that 2 flowers are fragrant and colourful. Region h is the part of them that is not fresh.

16
00:01:57,811 --> 00:02:05,641
The 2 flowers in the centre are part of item f. So region h has 2 minus 2, which is 0, flowers.

17
00:02:06,091 --> 00:02:14,603
Item e says that 5 flowers are fresh and colourful. 5 minus 2 is 3. So region i has 3 flowers.

18
00:02:15,053 --> 00:02:23,543
Item d says that 6 flowers are fresh and fragrant. 6 minus 2 is 4. So region j has 4 flowers.

19
00:02:23,993 --> 00:02:30,777
Now the regions with one property only. Item c says that the colourful circle has 8 flowers.

20
00:02:31,227 --> 00:02:42,278
Four regions make up this circle. Three of them are known: 2, 0 and 3, which add to 5. So region k has 8 minus 5, which is 3, flowers.

21
00:02:42,728 --> 00:02:55,997
Item b says that 7 flowers are fragrant. The known regions in this circle are 2, 0 and 4, which add to 6. So region l has 7 minus 6, which is 1, flower.

22
00:02:56,447 --> 00:03:09,418
Item a says that 11 flowers are fresh. The known regions in this circle are 2, 3 and 4, which add to 9. So region m has 11 minus 9, which is 2, flowers.

23
00:03:09,868 --> 00:03:20,897
The seven regions do not overlap, and together they make the bouquet. 2 plus 0 plus 3 plus 4 plus 3 plus 1 plus 2 is 15 flowers.

24
00:03:21,347 --> 00:03:31,139
The notes then give a shorter calculation. First, add the three single counts, a, b and c: 11 plus 7 plus 8 is 26.

25
00:03:31,589 --> 00:03:38,309
Then subtract the three pair counts, d, e and f: 6, 5 and 2, which is 13 in all.

26
00:03:38,759 --> 00:03:41,575
Then add the triple count, g, which is 2.

27
00:03:42,025 --> 00:03:47,935
26 minus 13 plus 2 is 15. This is the same answer as before.

28
00:03:48,385 --> 00:03:59,307
Here is why it works. We follow how many times each region is counted. After we add a, b and c, a region with one property is counted 1 time.

29
00:03:59,757 --> 00:04:05,155
A region with two properties is in two of the circles, so it is counted 2 times.

30
00:04:05,605 --> 00:04:09,957
The centre is in all three circles, so it is counted 3 times.

31
00:04:10,407 --> 00:04:20,903
Now subtract d, e and f. A region with two properties is in exactly one pair set. So it drops from 2 to 2 minus 1, which is 1.

32
00:04:21,353 --> 00:04:27,945
The centre is in all three pair sets. So it drops from 3 to 3 minus 3, which is 0.

33
00:04:28,395 --> 00:04:35,179
Add g. Only the centre is in the triple set. So it goes back to 0 plus 1, which is 1.

34
00:04:35,629 --> 00:04:42,199
Now every region is counted exactly once. So the short formula gives the number of flowers.

35
00:04:42,649 --> 00:04:47,705
With more sets, the regions are hard to draw. The short formula still works.

36
00:04:48,155 --> 00:05:00,145
Now the general theorem. Let A 1 to A m be finite sets. For a nonempty set S of indices, A sub S is the intersection of the sets A i with i in S.

37
00:05:00,595 --> 00:05:07,037
For example, A sub the set 2, 3, 5 is A 2 intersect A 3 intersect A 5.

38
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:15,423
For the flowers, m is 3. A 1 is the fresh flowers, A 2 the fragrant flowers, and A 3 the colourful flowers.

39
00:05:15,873 --> 00:05:26,092
The sets S of size 1 give A 1, A 2 and A 3. Items a, b and c give their sizes: 11, 7 and 8. Their sign is plus.

40
00:05:26,542 --> 00:05:36,121
The sets S of size 2 give the pair intersections. Items d, e and f give their sizes: 6, 5 and 2. Their sign is minus.

41
00:05:36,571 --> 00:05:44,293
The set 1, 2, 3 gives the triple intersection. Item g gives its size, 2. Its sign is plus again.

42
00:05:44,743 --> 00:05:51,506
Add the last column with its signs. The result is 15, the same as the short calculation.

43
00:05:51,956 --> 00:06:04,628
Theorem 1.15 says: the size of the union is the sum over all nonempty S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1, times the size of A sub S.

44
00:06:05,078 --> 00:06:14,102
There are 2 to the m minus 1 terms. For m equal to 3, that is 8 minus 1, which is 7, the rows of the table.

45
00:06:14,552 --> 00:06:19,459
Odd sizes of S get a plus sign. Even sizes get a minus sign.

46
00:06:19,909 --> 00:06:27,013
Why does the theorem work for any m? Take one element x. Say x is in exactly k of the sets.

47
00:06:27,463 --> 00:06:33,927
Then x is in A sub S exactly when every index in S is one of those k indices.

48
00:06:34,377 --> 00:06:38,601
Take k equal to 3: x is in A 1, A 2 and A 3.

49
00:06:39,051 --> 00:06:45,323
The three sets S of size 1 each count x once, with a plus sign. That gives plus 3.

50
00:06:45,773 --> 00:06:52,535
The three sets S of size 2 each count x once, with a minus sign. That gives minus 3.

51
00:06:52,985 --> 00:06:58,532
The set 1, 2, 3 counts x once more, with a plus sign. That gives plus 1.

52
00:06:58,982 --> 00:07:04,443
The total is 3 minus 3 plus 1, which is 1. So x is counted once.

53
00:07:04,893 --> 00:07:17,928
For any k, there are k choose j sets S of size j. So x is counted the sum, for j from 1 to k, of minus 1 to the j minus 1, times k choose j.

54
00:07:18,378 --> 00:07:23,306
The rows show this sum for k from 1 to 5. Each row adds to 1.

55
00:07:23,756 --> 00:07:29,921
By the Binomial Theorem, the sum is 1 minus the quantity 1 minus 1, to the k.

56
00:07:30,371 --> 00:07:41,657
1 minus 1 is 0, and 0 to the k is 0, because k is at least 1. So the sum is 1. Section 2.5 of the notes gives the full proof.

57
00:07:42,107 --> 00:07:52,069
An element in none of the sets is in no A sub S, so it is counted 0 times. Thus the sum counts each element of the union exactly once.

58
00:07:52,519 --> 00:08:01,223
Many problems ask for the objects with none of some bad properties. Each bad property gives a set: A 1, A 2 and A 3.

59
00:08:01,673 --> 00:08:05,151
Put all the sets inside one set U of all objects.

60
00:08:05,601 --> 00:08:09,355
The green region is the set of objects in none of the sets.

61
00:08:09,805 --> 00:08:14,072
Its size is the size of U minus the size of the union.

62
00:08:14,522 --> 00:08:17,487
Apply Theorem 1.15 to the union.

63
00:08:17,937 --> 00:08:27,943
Move the minus sign into the sum. Each sign changes: minus 1 to the size of S minus 1 becomes minus 1 to the size of S.

64
00:08:28,393 --> 00:08:38,697
Agree that A sub the empty set is U. Its term is plus the size of U. Then all terms form one sum over all subsets S of 1 to m.

65
00:08:39,147 --> 00:08:44,587
So the plan has three parts. First, name each bad property as a set A i.

66
00:08:45,037 --> 00:08:47,746
Second, count every intersection A sub S.

67
00:08:48,196 --> 00:08:54,767
Third, put the counts into this sum. The derangement problem later in this video uses this form.

68
00:08:55,217 --> 00:09:10,385
Here is an example to practise. How many integers from 1 to 100 are divisible by at least one of 2, 3 and 5? Let A 1, A 2 and A 3 be the multiples of 2, 3 and 5. Pause the video and try it.

69
00:09:10,835 --> 00:09:20,691
A 1 is the multiples of 2, with blue dots. Every second number is in A 1. 100 over 2 is 50, so A 1 has 50 elements.

70
00:09:21,141 --> 00:09:32,938
A 2 is the multiples of 3, with purple dots. 100 over 3 is 33 point 3. The floor brackets round down, so A 2 has 33 elements.

71
00:09:33,388 --> 00:09:41,985
A 3 is the multiples of 5, with orange dots. 100 over 5 is 20, so A 3 has 20 elements.

72
00:09:42,435 --> 00:09:51,673
Some cells have two or three dots. For example, 6 has two dots, and 30 has three dots. These numbers are in more than one set.

73
00:09:52,123 --> 00:10:05,669
A number is divisible by 2 and by 3 exactly when it is divisible by 6. 100 over 6 is 16 point 7. Round down: A 1 intersect A 2 has 16 elements.

74
00:10:06,119 --> 00:10:15,805
Divisible by 2 and by 5 means divisible by 10. 100 over 10 is 10. So A 1 intersect A 3 has 10 elements.

75
00:10:16,255 --> 00:10:26,132
Divisible by 3 and by 5 means divisible by 15. 100 over 15 is 6 point 7. Round down: 6 elements.

76
00:10:26,582 --> 00:10:38,401
Divisible by all three means divisible by 30. 100 over 30 is 3 point 3. Round down: 3 elements, the numbers 30, 60 and 90.

77
00:10:38,851 --> 00:10:48,387
By inclusion and exclusion, the answer is 50 plus 33 plus 20, minus 16, 10 and 6, plus 3. That is 74.

78
00:10:48,837 --> 00:10:58,373
The green cells are the 74 numbers. The gray cells have no dot. There are 100 minus 74, which is 26, of them.

79
00:10:58,823 --> 00:11:09,596
One more short example, with only two sets. How many binary strings of length 8 start with 1 1 or end with 0 0? Pause the video and try it.

80
00:11:10,046 --> 00:11:15,315
Let A 1 be the strings that start with 1 1. The first two positions are fixed.

81
00:11:15,765 --> 00:11:23,189
Each of the other 6 positions is 0 or 1. So A 1 has 2 to the 6, which is 64, strings.

82
00:11:23,639 --> 00:11:34,498
Let A 2 be the strings that end with 0 0. Its other 6 positions are free, so A 2 also has 2 to the 6, which is 64, strings.

83
00:11:34,948 --> 00:11:46,596
A string in both sets starts with 1 1 and ends with 0 0. The 4 middle positions are free, so the intersection has 2 to the 4, which is 16, strings.

84
00:11:47,046 --> 00:11:56,902
With two sets, the theorem says: the size of the union is the size of A 1, plus the size of A 2, minus the size of the intersection.

85
00:11:57,352 --> 00:12:03,432
So the answer is 64 plus 64 minus 16, which is 112.

86
00:12:03,882 --> 00:12:20,586
This is practice problem P1-7 from Assignment 1. A has three elements, a, b and c. B has five elements. Count all functions from A to B, the injective ones, and the surjective ones. Pause the video and try it.

87
00:12:21,036 --> 00:12:27,991
Part a. A function gives each element of A one value in B. Draw one arrow from each element of A.

88
00:12:28,441 --> 00:12:42,691
Each arrow can go to any of the 5 elements of B. So each of the three arrows has 5 choices. By the AND rule, there are 5 times 5 times 5, which is 125, functions.

89
00:12:43,141 --> 00:12:51,589
Part b. An injective function gives different elements different values. Here a, b and c go to 3, 1 and 4.

90
00:12:52,039 --> 00:13:05,437
The arrow from a has 5 choices. Then the arrow from b has 4 choices, and the arrow from c has 3. So there are 5 times 4 times 3, which is 60, injective functions.

91
00:13:05,887 --> 00:13:15,444
Part c. A surjective function reaches every element of B. But there are only 3 arrows, so at most 3 elements of B get an arrow.

92
00:13:15,894 --> 00:13:26,411
So at least 5 minus 3, which is 2, elements of B get no arrow. Here they are 4 and 5, in red. So there are 0 surjective functions.

93
00:13:26,861 --> 00:13:39,832
Inclusion and exclusion also counts surjections when the answer is not 0. Count the surjections from the set 1, 2, 3, 4 to the set 1, 2, 3. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:13:40,282 --> 00:13:49,071
Let U be all functions. Each of the 4 elements has 3 choices, so U has 3 to the 4, which is 81, functions.

95
00:13:49,521 --> 00:13:57,009
Let A i be the functions with no arrow to i. A function is surjective when it is in none of the sets A i.

96
00:13:57,459 --> 00:14:07,059
Take a set S of k values. A function in A sub S avoids these k values. It uses only the other 3 minus k values.

97
00:14:07,509 --> 00:14:15,445
Each of the 4 elements has 3 minus k choices. So the size of A sub S is 3 minus k, to the 4.

98
00:14:15,895 --> 00:14:20,845
This depends only on k. There are 3 choose k sets S of size k.

99
00:14:21,295 --> 00:14:30,895
Fill the table. Row k equal to 0 is the empty set S. It gives 1 times 3 to the 4, which is 81, with a plus sign.

100
00:14:31,345 --> 00:14:42,225
For k equal to 1: 3 sets, and 2 to the 4, which is 16, functions each. The term is minus 3 times 16, which is minus 48.

101
00:14:42,675 --> 00:14:49,885
For k equal to 2: 3 sets, and 1 to the 4, which is 1, function each. The term is plus 3.

102
00:14:50,335 --> 00:14:57,098
For k equal to 3: no value is left, and 0 to the 4 is 0. The term is 0.

103
00:14:57,548 --> 00:15:02,967
The sum is 81 minus 48 plus 3 minus 0, which is 36.

104
00:15:03,417 --> 00:15:09,262
A list of all 81 functions by computer also finds 36 surjections.

105
00:15:09,712 --> 00:15:20,699
As a check, use the same method for P1-7 part c. Now the domain is a, b, c, with 3 elements, and B has 5 elements.

106
00:15:21,149 --> 00:15:30,023
The terms are minus 1 to the k, times 5 choose k, times 5 minus k cubed, for k from 0 to 5.

107
00:15:30,473 --> 00:15:37,001
Row k equal to 0: 1 times 5 cubed is 125, with a plus sign.

108
00:15:37,451 --> 00:15:46,646
Row k equal to 1: 5 times 4 cubed, which is 5 times 64, is 320, with a minus sign.

109
00:15:47,096 --> 00:15:55,629
Row k equal to 2: 10 times 3 cubed, which is 10 times 27, is 270, with a plus sign.

110
00:15:56,079 --> 00:16:03,183
Row k equal to 3: 10 times 2 cubed, which is 10 times 8, is 80, with a minus sign.

111
00:16:03,633 --> 00:16:13,809
Row k equal to 4 gives plus 5 times 1, which is 5. Row k equal to 5 gives 0, because 0 cubed is 0.

112
00:16:14,259 --> 00:16:23,603
The sum is 125 minus 320 plus 270 minus 80 plus 5 minus 0, which is 0.

113
00:16:24,053 --> 00:16:30,411
This agrees with the direct argument. Use the direct argument on an exam when it is shorter.

114
00:16:30,861 --> 00:16:41,335
Now the classical application in Section 1.2.5. Eight people check their coats at a restaurant. The coats come back in a random order.

115
00:16:41,785 --> 00:16:48,207
What is the probability that no person gets the correct coat? We solve it for any number n of people.

116
00:16:48,657 --> 00:16:52,305
List the people P 1 to P n. Here n is 8.

117
00:16:52,755 --> 00:17:01,437
Record the result as a sequence c 1 to c n. c i equals j means that person i got the coat of person j.

118
00:17:01,887 --> 00:17:09,013
Each number from 1 to n appears exactly once. So the sequence is a permutation of 1 to n.

119
00:17:09,463 --> 00:17:18,487
Person i gets the correct coat exactly when c i equals i. Here c 2 equals 2, so person 2 gets the correct coat.

120
00:17:18,937 --> 00:17:24,078
A permutation with c i different from i for every i is a derangement.

121
00:17:24,528 --> 00:17:28,965
Let A i be the set of permutations with c i equal to i.

122
00:17:29,415 --> 00:17:36,157
The union of the sets A i is the set of permutations where at least one person gets the correct coat.

123
00:17:36,607 --> 00:17:47,977
So the number of derangements is n factorial minus the size of the union. This is the complement form, with U the set of all n factorial permutations.

124
00:17:48,427 --> 00:17:57,323
We need the size of every A sub S. The notes use the example n equal to 8, and S equal to the set 2, 3, 6.

125
00:17:57,773 --> 00:18:05,005
A permutation in A sub S has 2 in position 2, 3 in position 3, and 6 in position 6.

126
00:18:05,455 --> 00:18:09,125
The stars are the other 5 positions. They are free.

127
00:18:09,575 --> 00:18:21,095
The numbers 1, 4, 5, 7 and 8 fill the five free positions in some order. Each order is a list of these five numbers. Here is one of them in green.

128
00:18:21,545 --> 00:18:37,353
There are 5 factorial lists of five numbers. 5 factorial is 5 times 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, which is 120. So A sub the set 2, 3, 6 has 120 permutations.

129
00:18:37,803 --> 00:18:54,123
In general, if S has k elements, fix the k positions in S. The other n minus k numbers form a list in the other positions. So the size of A sub S is n minus k, factorial. Here 8 minus 3 is 5.

130
00:18:54,573 --> 00:18:59,714
This depends only on k, and there are n choose k sets S of size k.

131
00:19:00,164 --> 00:19:08,207
Now apply Theorem 1.15 to the derangements. Group the sets S by their size k, from 1 to n.

132
00:19:08,657 --> 00:19:18,577
Each of the n choose k sets of size k gives the same term: minus 1 to the k minus 1, times n minus k, factorial.

133
00:19:19,027 --> 00:19:30,568
Simplify n choose k times n minus k factorial. Write n choose k as n factorial over k factorial times n minus k factorial.

134
00:19:31,018 --> 00:19:38,442
The factor n minus k factorial cancels. What is left is n factorial over k factorial.

135
00:19:38,892 --> 00:19:46,209
So the union has n factorial times the sum of minus 1 to the k minus 1, over k factorial.

136
00:19:46,659 --> 00:19:51,737
The number of derangements is n factorial minus the size of the union.

137
00:19:52,187 --> 00:20:09,531
Move n factorial into the sum. Each sign changes, and n factorial becomes the term for k equal to 0. The result is n factorial times the sum, for k from 0 to n, of minus 1 to the k over k factorial.

138
00:20:09,981 --> 00:20:22,354
The k equal to 0 term is n factorial times 1 over 0 factorial, which is n factorial. It is the term for the empty set S, which is the size of U.

139
00:20:22,804 --> 00:20:27,860
Check with n equal to 4. Each row of the table is one value of k.

140
00:20:28,310 --> 00:20:36,395
k equal to 0: 4 choose 0 is 1, and 4 factorial is 24. The term is plus 24.

141
00:20:36,845 --> 00:20:44,675
k equal to 1: 4 times 3 factorial, which is 4 times 6, is 24. The sign is minus.

142
00:20:45,125 --> 00:20:52,378
k equal to 2: 6 times 2 factorial, which is 6 times 2, is 12. The sign is plus.

143
00:20:52,828 --> 00:20:58,311
k equal to 3: 4 times 1 factorial is 4. The sign is minus.

144
00:20:58,761 --> 00:21:04,307
k equal to 4: 1 times 0 factorial is 1. The sign is plus.

145
00:21:04,757 --> 00:21:10,603
The sum is 24 minus 24 plus 12 minus 4 plus 1, which is 9.

146
00:21:11,053 --> 00:21:22,786
A computer list of all 24 permutations of 1, 2, 3, 4 finds exactly these 9 derangements. In each one, no number is in its own position.

147
00:21:23,236 --> 00:21:32,857
All n factorial permutations are equally likely. So the probability of a derangement is the number of derangements over n factorial.

148
00:21:33,307 --> 00:21:40,177
This is D n, the sum of minus 1 to the k over k factorial, for k from 0 to n.

149
00:21:40,627 --> 00:21:51,315
The table shows some exact values. For n equal to 4, there are 9 derangements, so D 4 is 9 over 24, which is 3 over 8.

150
00:21:51,765 --> 00:21:55,199
The blue points show D n for n from 0 to 10.

151
00:21:55,649 --> 00:22:01,004
The sum is the start of the series for e to the x, at x equal to minus 1.

152
00:22:01,454 --> 00:22:10,819
So D n approaches 1 over e, which is about 0.367879. The purple line marks this value.

153
00:22:11,269 --> 00:22:15,259
From n equal to 7 on, D n changes very little.

154
00:22:15,709 --> 00:22:39,623
For the eight people of Example 1.23, there are 14833 derangements, and 8 factorial is 40320. So D 8 is 14833 over 40320, which is 2119 over 5760.

155
00:22:40,073 --> 00:22:46,836
That is about 0.367882, or about 36.79 percent.

156
00:22:47,286 --> 00:22:58,763
A last practice question. How many lists of 1 to 5 have 1 in position 1, or 2 in position 2, or 3 in position 3? Pause the video and try it.

157
00:22:59,213 --> 00:23:07,469
Let A i be the lists with i in position i, for i equal to 1, 2 and 3. We want the size of their union.

158
00:23:07,919 --> 00:23:18,117
Fix 1 in position 1. The other four numbers form a list in the other four positions. So A 1 has 4 factorial, which is 24, lists.

159
00:23:18,567 --> 00:23:27,591
A 2 and A 3 also have 24 lists each. So the three sets of size 1 give 3 times 24, which is 72.

160
00:23:28,041 --> 00:23:37,321
Fix two positions, for example 1 and 2. Three numbers are free, so each pair intersection has 3 factorial, which is 6, lists.

161
00:23:37,771 --> 00:23:42,955
There are 3 pairs of sets, so the pairs give 3 times 6, which is 18.

162
00:23:43,405 --> 00:23:50,957
Fix all three positions. Two numbers are free, so the triple intersection has 2 factorial, which is 2, lists.

163
00:23:51,407 --> 00:23:55,439
Here is the written solution. First, define the sets A i.

164
00:23:55,889 --> 00:23:59,003
Second, give the size of A sub S, with a reason.

165
00:23:59,453 --> 00:24:05,512
Third, name the theorem and compute: 72 minus 18 plus 2 is 56.

166
00:24:05,962 --> 00:24:11,061
A computer list of all 120 lists also finds 56.

167
00:24:11,511 --> 00:24:17,441
To recap. When sets overlap, the sum of their sizes counts shared elements more than once.

168
00:24:17,891 --> 00:24:31,353
Theorem 1.15 corrects this with alternating signs. An element in k of the sets is counted 1 minus the quantity 1 minus 1 to the k times, which is 1 time.

169
00:24:31,803 --> 00:24:38,864
To count the objects in none of the sets, use the complement form, with A sub the empty set equal to U.

170
00:24:39,314 --> 00:24:47,762
When the size of A sub S depends only on the size k of S, call it N k. Then group the terms with m choose k.

171
00:24:48,212 --> 00:24:56,297
This gives the count of surjections. For P1-7, the answers are 125, 60 and 0.

172
00:24:56,747 --> 00:25:11,553
It also gives the count of derangements. The probability of a derangement approaches 1 over e. The next video, 5 b, uses counting to find probabilities: birthdays, Wine Gums and poker hands.
